The plots below show five 2D data sets, each with two Gaussian classes with different means (class 1 left, class 2 right).
The table gives their covariance matrices $\Sigma_1$ and $\Sigma_2$.
For each case, decide which of the classifiers LDA, QDA and Gaussian NB can model the data and which cannot, and justify your answer via the covariance structure.

<<covariance-structures-ex, echo = FALSE, message = FALSE, warning = FALSE, fig.width = 8, fig.height = 4.3, out.width = "\\textwidth">>=
source("../rsrc/covariance-structures.R")
panels = plot_cov_cases(paste("Case", 1:5))
wrap_plots(panels[1:2]) / wrap_plots(panels[3:5]) + plot_layout(heights = c(1.5, 1))
@

{\centering
\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|c|c|c|}
\hline
 & \textbf{Case 1} & \textbf{Case 2} & \textbf{Case 3} & \textbf{Case 4} & \textbf{Case 5} \\
\hline
\rule[-3.4ex]{0pt}{8ex}$\Sigma_1$ & $\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 1 \\ 1 & 2 \end{pmatrix}$ & $\begin{pmatrix} 3 & 2 \\ 2 & 3 \end{pmatrix}$ & $\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix}$ & $\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix}$ & $\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 0 & 2 \end{pmatrix}$ \\
\hline
\rule[-3.4ex]{0pt}{8ex}$\Sigma_2$ & $\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 1 \\ 1 & 2 \end{pmatrix}$ & $\begin{pmatrix} 3 & -2 \\ -2 & 2 \end{pmatrix}$ & $\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix}$ & $\begin{pmatrix} 3 & 0 \\ 0 & 3 \end{pmatrix}$ & $\begin{pmatrix} 3 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix}$ \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\par}
